  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/1/19/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_45.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Iron Man rushes toward the Indianapolis 500, where Tony Stark is scheduled to drive the \"Stark Special.\"&nbsp; But during the race, his heart -- he forgot to recharge his chest plate -- threatens to give out!&nbsp; His car crashes, and he's pinned underneath.&nbsp; To all observers, it appears that Stark is a goner.&nbsp; But suddenly, a burly man rushes out of the crowd and hauls him out of the car.&nbsp; Stark offers to pay him a handsome sum if he rushes him to the nearest motel and lock him in a room by himself -- no questions asked.&nbsp; The mysterious stranger does indeed help him to a room, where Stark then plugs in his chest plate and revives.&nbsp; A grateful Tony Stark intends to reward the \"grouchy stranger\" who pulled him out of the wreck.\n\nA few minutes later, in the motel lounge, Stark treats the large man to a meal and learns that his name is Harry Hogan, nicknamed \"Happy\" by the folks at Stillman's Gym, \"mostly 'cause no one's ever caught me with a smile.\"&nbsp; He's a former (and unsuccessful) boxer looking for steady work, and so when Stark hands him a check for 50 thousand dollars, he tears it up and asks instead for a steady job.\n\nThinking that it might not be a bad idea to have a chauffeur/bodyguard, Stark hires him on the spot as his driver.&nbsp; This is more to Happy's liking, especially when he realizes the types of <i>cars</i> Stark rides around in, namely rides like a Rolls Royce, Caddy Eldorado and Jaguar convertible.&nbsp; Stark hands him a ten-thousand dollar check for odds and ends he'll need on the job, and a few days later, back in New York, the arrangement is finalized.\n\nThe pair head to Stark's plant in Flushing, Long Island, where Stark introduces him to his secretary, \"Pepper\" Potts (whose real name must be Kitty if page 8 is to be believed).&nbsp; Happy is immediately stricken with her, but she has eyes only for Tony.&nbsp; The two instantly begin bickering.\n\nMeanwhile, Stark ducks into his private office and changes to Iron Man to test out his suit, but soon he sees someone breaking into an underground vault.&nbsp; Moments later, he uses one of the hidden passageways to sneak up on \"Professor Shapanka, one of my most trusted scientists!\"&nbsp; He was trying to rob the vault -- particularly to steal the secret of Stark's tiny transistors -- not counting on Iron Man being there.&nbsp; He actually shoots a gun at Iron Man, but is ultimately apprehended.\n\nTony actually lets him walk because of his past brilliant work.&nbsp; Shapanka storms off, still wanting to achieve \"eternal life.\"&nbsp; He knows that freezing preserves things like meat, but he wants to be awake while his body is frozen, so he'll build a special cold suit and become <b>Jack Frost</b>!&nbsp; It will even allow him to freeze things near him, all while protecting him from the icy cold.\n\nHe's able to do this within a week, and arrives, as if on cue, at a bank to pick up some \"cold cash.\"&nbsp; Sure enough, with the aid of the cold gun that's part of his suit, he freezes people and robs the bank.&nbsp; He can't wait for another meeting with both Stark and Iron Man.\n\nSoon, he makes a brazen attack on the Stark Industries plant and manages to freeze both Pepper and Happy as he advances toward Stark's inner office.&nbsp; Naturally, Iron Man is there to greet him.&nbsp; Iron Man even recognizes Shapanka's voice, and before Jack Frost can fully unleash his cold powers on him, Iron Man uses the devices in his suit to make a mini blast-furnace that melts the ice.&nbsp; The villain's powers are effectively neutralized, and Frost is quickly subdued, de-iced, and handed over to the police.\n\nBoth Happy and Pepper are on hand to see things get wrapped up, and Happy even thinks that it's a good thing he's around, because it \"looks like Stark needs a heap of protecting... and Iron Man may not always be here!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "A steady, worthwhile issue whose real value lies in establishing Stark Industries, Happy, and Pepper -- not in the plug‑and‑play villain driving the plot.",
  "subtitle": "Cold on Thrills, Warm on World-Building",
  "review_text": "The plot involving Jack Frost was largely mundane, offering little beyond a functional excuse to showcase the new supporting characters of Happy and Pepper -- which is even mentioned on the cover (in a box that is actually bigger than the one touting Iron Man's latest adversary).\n\n[ASSET: left | HappyHogan | width=18%] [ASSET: right | StarkIndustriesLocations | width=11%] Happy is introduced first, and after he rescues Stark from certain death in the Indianapolis 500, he is quickly established as Stark's driver in the book's first 6+ pages.\n[BR]\n[BR] We also learn here that Stark's main research plant is in Flushing, Long Island, but that he also has plants all over the world (\"one on each continent\" even, which seems a bit unbelievable -- even <i>Antarctica</i>?)\n\nNaturally, the pair hit it off right away... [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=not!]\n[BR] (Their instant friction is practically the <i>point</i>.)\n[ASSET: center | BickeringBegins | width=67%]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And Happy is at least right in the fact that Stan <i>clearly</i> has added them to the cast in order to start a love triangle...and to <b><i>bicker</i></b>!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | Clanking | width=12%] [ASSET: right | ColdFeet | width=11%] The story also introduces the notion of Stark having a private office where he can freely test out his Iron Man abilities.&nbsp; Not only that, there is a labyrinth of secret passages that he can use to quickly get to different places around his plant.\n[BR]\n[BR] What I also found interesting (even though there is <i>zero</i> lip service given to it) was the fact that, as Iron Man runs down a corridor, we see his feet making [SOUNDEFFECT: text=CLANK! CLANK!] sound effects.\n[BR]\n[BR] (I guess Iron Man won't ever be sneaking up on anyone, <i>heh</i>, especially not with all that <i>racket</i>!)\n[BR]\n[BR] Professor Shapanka is discovered trying to rob Stark's vault, and Tony unwisely releases him without any consequences, but in the process gives him the idea of using cold to extend life.\n\nAfterward, the notion of cold leads to the creation of Shapanka's new identity as \"Jack Frost\":\n[ASSET: center | JackFrost | width=67%]\n\nBy this time, however, the story seems to have done what Stan intended to do, which was to add this bit of world-building, and the reader never again senses that this new Jack Frost character is anything other than a way to fill out the second half of the story, and even <i>that half</i> feels more like connective tissue than true conflict, for when he finally returns to Stark Industries, the story goes out of its way to get Pepper and Happy involved (both get frozen, as shown on the cover).\n\nClearly, they were more important to the actual contents of the issue than Jack Frost ever was, because the final conflict between him and Iron Man is over within about three pages.\n\nWas anyone surprised?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I'm betting <i>not</i>!)]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nMillionaire playboy or not, I seriously question Tony Stark driving a vehicle in as dangerous a race as the Indianapolis 500, and then doing so without first ensuring that his chest plate was fully <i>charged</i>?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Come on now!&nbsp; Yeesh!]\n\nSuch a questionable plot element like this was obviously just staged in order to bring in the new supporting character of Happy Hogan.\n\nEven though this was clumsily done, what the book does <i>right</i> is to finally give Tony Stark/Iron Man a true base of operations -- Stark Industries -- and this Flushing, Long Island plant.&nbsp; Up until now, his adventures have bounced from locale to locale with little sense of a central hub.\n\nWith a character like Stark, who owns an international company, you'd <i>expect</i> stories to span the globe, so there is no real problem with what we've been treated to thus far, but now that the character is a half-dozen stories in, Stan does a good job finally giving him a home base, which is a staple of the other magazines (e.g. the Fantastic Four's Baxter Building, Dr. Blake's medical practice, Peter Parker's Midtown High, etc.).\n\nThe supporting characters may have been introduced in a haphazard manner, and that is indeed the case here.&nbsp; The klaxon-style cover announcement of:\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Lookie at what we have here -- SUPPORTING CHARACTERS!&nbsp; <i>Woohoo</i>!]\n\nIt's a bit much, but nevertheless, having them is important to the long-term success of the feature, so in that regard, I applaud Stan for adding Happy and Pepper to the mix.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But tone down the billboard a bit, <i>okay</i>?)]\n\n[ASSET: right | GadgetManipulation | width=11%] The story does a couple of <i>other</i> things right as well.&nbsp; The continued use of Iron Man manipulating the various gadgets on his suit is one of the magazine's recurring strengths I've already noted, and artist Don Heck provides a couple of close-ups, such as this one from page 18.\n[BR]\n[BR] But perhaps more importantly going forward is the look of determination on Happy's face in the final panel -- one who seems <i>determined</i> to be on hand to protect his new boss, realizing that Stark's position as head of a global company puts a veritable target on his back, setting up a dynamic that could complicate Stark's double life.\n[BR]\n[BR] He even thinks, \"Iron Man may not always be here!\"\n\nThis bodes well for the magazine because it promises to introduce an ongoing problem for Stark in the future, forcing Tony to give Happy the slip from time to time in order to change into Iron Man (and vice versa).\n\nAs for Jack Frost, however... as I said above, he was merely fodder for the supporting characters, and even though he <i>did</i> dominate the last half of the book (which features his origin), one never gets the impression that he was anything more than an incidental afterthought in a story that seemed far more interested in establishing the two new supporting characters (and even <i>said</i> so on the cover).\n\nWill we ever see Jack Frost again?&nbsp; I don't know...\n[BR]But perhaps more <i>important</i>... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=does it even <i>matter</i>?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(His <i>real</i> purpose has already been served.)]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] Noted on the cover is that the Iron Man part of the book has now been extended to 18 pages.&nbsp; Still not quite a full-sized book, but one of the backup tales has been dropped to give Iron Man more room.\n\n[HANG0] Page 7 makes a point to indicate that Stark's factory located near the \"new baseball stadium and the site of the World's Fair.\"&nbsp; That would be the 1964 World's Fair, making this Shea Stadium, which opened in April, 1964 (and later demolished in 2009).&nbsp; The New York Jets also played there, as did the Beatles on a couple of occasions."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "By the time I started reading <i>Iron Man</i> in 1977, Happy and Pepper had largely disappeared from the book, so it was fun to see them be introduced, particularly now that they've been given new life in the MCU movies.",
  "highlight": "Cold villain, lukewarm adventure, but Iron Man's world finally starts to thaw into shape."
}